  "slug": "splicing-maturation-ef7f",
  "summary": "Fresh preprints and a J Cell Biol report add direct perturbation and phase-behavior data to nuclear RNA maturation timing.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that nuclear RNA maturation depends on the timing and molecular composition of processing steps. In Drosophila spermatocytes, Y-loop A co-transcriptional splicing is organized in chromatin hubs, with delays that vary by transcription cluster and increase as spermatocytes mature. In yeast, nuclear mRNPs share a minimal Yra1p/Pab1p core, while most factors associate transiently in stage-specific modules shaped by intron class and promoter. What is new is direct perturbation: the covalent molecular glue HL-1B stabilizes TOE1 binding to the spliceosome, altering snRNA 3'-processing and splicing in human cells. The zebrafish fractionation study links RNA insolubility to ribosome, poly(A) tail and m6A features, but addresses phase behavior rather than splicing, and the SF3b1 abscission work connects splicing to cytokinesis. The shared thread is therefore strongest for timing and composition rather than a unified mechanism.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-23"
}
